//go:build tools

// Package tools pins the module dependencies that generated code requires.
//
// Why this file exists:
//
// `proto/echo.proto` imports `mcp/jsonschema/jsonschema.proto`. When you run
// `make generate` (buf generate), the emitted `proto/gen/echo.pb.go` imports:
//
//	_         "github.com/sunerpy/protoc-gen-jsonschema/mcp/jsonschema"
//	protoimpl "google.golang.org/protobuf/runtime/protoimpl"
//
// Those imports only appear AFTER codegen runs. `go mod tidy` prunes any module
// that no checked-in source imports, so without this file it would strip both
// modules from go.mod, and the very next `go build ./...` after `make generate`
// would fail with "no required module provides package ...".
//
// The `//go:build tools` constraint keeps this file out of every normal build,
// test, and binary — it contributes zero code. But `go mod tidy` deliberately
// evaluates source files as if ALL build tags were enabled (the only exception
// is the `ignore` tag), so these blank imports are enough to keep both modules
// in the `require` block permanently, independent of whether `proto/gen/` has
// been generated yet.
//
// This is the standard Go `tools.go` pattern. Keep this file; deleting it makes
// the module dependencies disappear on the next `go mod tidy`.
//
// If you drop the proto/JSON Schema workflow entirely, delete this file, the
// `proto/` directory, `buf.*.yaml`, and the Makefile `generate` target together,
// then run `go mod tidy` once.
package tools

import (
	// Runtime support for the JSON Schema proto extensions used by proto/echo.proto.
	_ "github.com/sunerpy/protoc-gen-jsonschema/mcp/jsonschema"
	// Runtime support required by every protoc-gen-go generated .pb.go file.
	_ "google.golang.org/protobuf/runtime/protoimpl"
)
